    The final part of my presentation is about different strategies of detecting unused code.

22:
    Unused code detection is a complex task.
    Both Vulture and Deadcode uses abstract syntax tree to parse and analyse Python code.
    However, Abstract Syntax Tree does not provide information about association of
    a definition and its useage.
    This meta information has to be somehow constructed.

23:
    The strategy used by vulture executes three steps.
    First it finds all defined names.
    Second it finds all used names.
    Third it reports the difference between these two sets.

The result will be empty set, nothing will be reported.

24:
Lets complicate this example a little bit by adding additional class name Bar.
with a signle method spam.
As we can see two different classes have the same method, but only Foo.spam() method is used.

In the first step vulture will find:
    three defined names: Foo, spam an Bar.
In the second step the same used names will be found:
    Foo, spam and Bar
And nothing will be reported, even though Bar.spam method is not used.
This is because a namespace of a code item is not taken into account.


25:
    Now lets consider the same example with a different strategy,
    when the namespace is being taken into account.

    In the first step, Foo.spam() and Bar.spam() methods will be detected as separate definitions.
    For the second step, only Foo.spam() usage will be detected.
    As a result Bar.spam() will be reported as unused.

    Sometimes it is hard or not possible to construct the namespace of a used code item.
    In such cases deadcode will fallback to the previous strategy comparing names
    without their namespaces.

26:
    This more precice code detection strategy has a nice side effect, which allows to 
    prune Python code tree to keeping only what is needed. 

    This allows to reduce the size of Python code bundle,
    which might be useful when size matters.

    For example when python code has to be served in the browser.


